.TH MAKI-CHECK 8
.SH NAME
maki-check \- check an offline Maki volume backing directory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B maki-check
.I volume-backing-root
.RB [ \-\-deep ]
.RB [ \-\-journal-segment-size
.IR bytes ]
.br
.BR "maki-check \-\-help" " | " \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
The default check inspects the on-disk metadata.
Pass the backing directory itself.
.B maki-check
reads no configuration, so it does not compare one with the volume. To check
by volume configuration, which also reports each setting fixed at creation
whose configured value differs from the volume's (attach refuses each one),
use
.BR "maki check" .
Without a configuration it cannot open a rollback-protected backing either,
whose witness only the configuration names: it refuses such a root, and
.B maki check
checks it, read-only where it cannot be written.
On Linux, a root the user running
.B maki-check
may not write (a read-only mount, a read-only bind mount, a directory without
write permission for that user, an immutable one) is opened read-only:
nothing is created, written, synced or removed, the report adds a
.B "warning: read-only check:"
line, and the deep check takes the volume lock shared and reports
.B "deep check verdict: unproven"
where a writable one says
.BR clean " or " recoverable .
.TP
.B \-\-deep
Acquire the volume lock and inspect checkpoint state, key canary, journal and
data slots. Detach the volume before running this check. On a root that
cannot be written the lock is taken shared and a missing
.I volume.lock
fails the check, since it cannot be created there.
.TP
.BI \-\-journal-segment-size " bytes"
Accepted for compatibility. The deep check bounds each journal segment file by
the largest file a valid configuration can produce (a
.B journal_segment_size
of at most 1 GiB), so no value within that limit changes the verdict. The
default is 268435456 bytes.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage line and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates a successful check. A failing check or I/O error returns 1.
A check that finds no error returns zero whatever its deep verdict: zero does
not mean
.BR clean .
A deep check reporting
.BR recoverable ,
or, on a root that cannot be written,
.BR unproven ,
returns zero too; a script that needs
.B clean
reads the
.B "info: deep check verdict:"
line. Invalid command usage returns 2.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR maki (8),
.BR maki-attach (8)
